    question:'A coral reef runs round much of the coast, a short way offshore. Inside it the water is shallow and calm; outside it the waves break heavily.<br>Why did fishing villages grow along the lagoon shore rather than on open coasts?',
    options:['The reef breaks the waves, so small boats can land and work in safety','Fish live only inside lagoons and are never found in the open sea beyond','Lagoon water is deeper, which lets far larger boats be moored close in','Open coasts were kept empty by law so that ships could pass freely'],
